### Usage/token categories (attempt.rs:127-166)

```rust
/// Distinct token categories. Every field stays `None` (never a fabricated
/// zero) until the provider reports it.
pub struct UsageCategories {
    pub uncached_input_tokens: Option<u64>,
    pub cache_read_tokens: Option<u64>,
    pub cache_write_tokens: Option<u64>,
    pub output_tokens: Option<u64>,
    pub reasoning_output_tokens: Option<u64>,
}

impl UsageCategories {
    /// Combine two partial observations without ever turning "unknown" into
    /// a fabricated zero: a category stays `None` only while both sides are.
    pub fn merge(&self, other: &UsageCategories) -> UsageCategories { ... }
    pub fn is_entirely_unknown(&self) -> bool { ... }
}
```
This maps 1:1 onto PRD-059's acceptance criteria ("input, output, cache-read, and cache-creation token categories") — `uncached_input_tokens`↔input, `cache_read_tokens`↔cache-read, `cache_write_tokens`↔cache-creation, `output_tokens`↔output. `reasoning_output_tokens` exists but per the PRD-059 design note, Anthropic doesn't expose a separate reasoning category — it's billed inside `output_tokens`, so the adapter should leave `reasoning_output_tokens` `None` rather than fabricate a split.

### Adapter-level stop reason (closed, small set) — attempt.rs:168-175

```rust
pub enum AdapterStopReason {
    EndTurn,
    ToolUse,
    MaxTokens,
    StopSequence,
    ContentFilter,
}
```
This is the **adapter's own** stop-reason enum (provider-facing), distinct from the loop's `StopReason` (below). `raw_runtime.rs:956-968` shows exactly how the loop maps `AdapterStopReason` → loop `StopReason` when there are no pending tool calls:
```rust
match outcome.stop_reason {
    A::EndTurn | A::ContentFilter | A::StopSequence => { ... StopReason::Completed { .. } }
    A::MaxTokens => break StopReason::TokenOrContextCeiling,
    A::ToolUse => break StopReason::InvalidStructuredOutput,
}
```
PRD-059 must map Anthropic's `end_turn`/`max_tokens`/`stop_sequence`/`tool_use`/`refusal`/`pause_turn` (or whatever the current API's set is) onto this closed 5-value enum. Anthropic's "refusal" maps to `ContentFilter` here (there's no dedicated `Refusal` variant); "pause-class continuation" per the PRD design note should presumably continue the loop rather than stop (needs verification against current API surface at implementation time — the PRD explicitly flags "re-verify at implementation").

### Loop-level closed stop reason set — `raw_runtime.rs:605-625`

```rust
pub enum ProviderFailureTaxonomy { Retryable, NonRetryable, Ambiguous }

/// The closed, honest stop-reason set. A failed stage is always its own
/// failure — never relabeled as token exhaustion by bookkeeping.
pub enum StopReason {
    Completed { structured_output: bool },
    IterationCeiling,
    TokenOrContextCeiling,
    BudgetStop,
    Timeout,
    Cancelled,
    ProviderFailure { taxonomy: ProviderFailureTaxonomy },
    FatalToolRefusal,
    InvalidStructuredOutput,
}
```

### Error taxonomy (closed) — attempt.rs:188-223

```rust
pub enum RetryableKind {
    RateLimited { retry_after_ms: Option<u64> },
    Overloaded,
    TransientTransport,
}

pub enum NonRetryableKind { Auth, InvalidRequest, RefusedContent }

/// `Ambiguous` is the honest timeout case: the provider may have accepted,
/// executed, and billed a request whose response never arrived, so usage for
/// that attempt stays pending/unknown rather than zero.
pub enum AdapterError {
    Retryable(RetryableKind),
    NonRetryable(NonRetryableKind),
    Ambiguous { reason: String },
}
```

